export async function checkout(user: User, cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  console.log("checkout start", user);
  try {
    const order = await createOrder(user, cart);
    console.log("checkout ok", user.email, order);
    return order;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("checkout failed", error, user, cart);
    return failedOrder();
  }
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The current code has three failure modes: it logs entire objects (user, cart, order) with no schema, so lines cannot be filtered or correlated; it transports personal data such as user.email, plus whatever cart and order happen to contain, with no redaction; and it emits thin, unstructured lines whose severity does not reflect actionability. There is also a hidden observability gap: the error is caught and swallowed, so it never reaches the SDK's automatic handlers and is invisible to error tracking.

Refactor, keeping behavior identical:

export async function checkout(user: User, cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  const startedAt = performance.now();
  try {
    const order = await createOrder(user, cart);
    Sentry.logger.info("Checkout completed", {
      order_id: order.id,
      item_count: cart.items.length,
      duration_ms: performance.now() - startedAt,
    });
    return order;
  } catch (error) {
    Sentry.logger.error("Checkout failed", {
      item_count: cart.items.length,
      duration_ms: performance.now() - startedAt,
    });
    Sentry.captureException(error);
    return failedOrder();
  }
}

Changes: stable fields (order_id, item_count, duration_ms) replace whole-object dumps; user.email and free-form payloads are dropped, redacting sensitive attributes before transport; one wide event per operation replaces the start/ok/failed triple that nobody joins during an incident; severity now reflects actionability, an error someone may need to act on versus an informational completion; captureException reports the handled failure to error tracking with the operation's context; and order_id lets an engineer move from the log to its trace, error, or replay. Ownership and retention for this log data are defined separately from the client API, because these events carry business data such as order ids and volumes.

code.code:
export async function checkout(cart: Cart): Promise<Order> {
  const span = tracer.startSpan("checkout");
  const order = await createOrder(cart);
  span.end();
  return order;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
When createOrder rejects, the rejection propagates before span.end() runs, so the span is never ended: the trace shows a checkout span with no duration (an unfinished, leaked span), and the error is not recorded on it, so the trace cannot explain the failure it was meant to cover. On the success path the span works, which is exactly what makes this bug easy to miss in reviews. Rewrite so the span ends on every path and the exception is recorded:

await tracer.startActiveSpan("checkout", async (span) => {
  try {
    return await createOrder(cart);
  } catch (error) {
    span.recordException(error);
    throw error;
  } finally {
    span.end();
  }
});

The finally block guarantees the span ends whether the operation succeeds, rejects, or throws synchronously. recordException attaches the failure to the span so the trace explains the error rather than merely timing it. Using the active-span form keeps the span context available to child operations, such as the outgoing request inside createOrder, so they attach to this trace instead of starting their own. One span covers one operation with a stable name, which keeps traces comparable across releases.
